        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> What's the use case for them staying open?\r\n\r\nMostly two things:\r\n- Robustly tracking lost focus for a window is not trivial. The best \"easy\" approach is to have it tracked inside autocommand for `WinLeave`, but there is no way to create autocommand local to a window, only buffer. There is now a 'winfixbuf' option to probably make these window-buffer a one-to-one relation, but it was introduced relatively recently (in 0.10.0).\r\n- The window [can be configured](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/e50cf9de614500a20e47cfc50e30a100042f91c3/doc/mini-test.txt#L646-L650) to open in a regular split. Having window closed after lost focus in this case is not a good idea (because regular split is probably used to be more persistent). And in turn, having different behavior for custom and default windows also doesn't seem like a good idea.\r\n\r\nYou can have this set up by tweaking the default `buffer` reporter like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal test = require('mini.test')\r\nlocal win_config = {\r\n  relative = 'editor',\r\n  width = math.floor(0.618 * vim.o.columns),\r\n  height = math.floor(0.618 * vim.o.lines),\r\n  row = math.floor(0.191 * vim.o.lines),\r\n  col = math.floor(0.191 * vim.o.columns),\r\n}\r\nlocal reporter = test.gen_reporter.buffer({\r\n  window = function()\r\n    local win_id = vim.api.nvim_open_win(0, true, win_config)\r\n    local au_opts = { once = true, callback = function() pcall(vim.api.nvim_win_close, win_id, true) end }\r\n    vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('WinEnter', au_opts)\r\n  end,\r\n})\r\ntest.setup({ execute = { reporter = reporter } })\r\n```\r\n\r\nClosing as an expected behavior.",
